11.text:
Western blot separates proteins by molecular weight so that two isoforms that share an epitope can be resolved by their mobility shift. DNMT3A1 and DNMT3A2 are N-terminal splice variants that are identical in their C-terminal catalytic domain and therefore comigrate as ~130 kDa and ~100 kDa species, respectively. DNMT3B1 and DNMT3B3 are N-terminally shared splice variants,  ~95- 96 kDa full length and ~  50-70 kDa truncated, respectively, which are identical in their N-terminal regulatory region and divergent at the C-terminus. DNMT3L is a separate, catalytically inactive family member ~ 43 kDa with essentially no cross-reactive linear epitope with DNMT3A/B.

Hence in practice

* one pan-DNMT3A antibody directed to an epitope common to DNMT3A1 and DNMT3A2, e.g. the conserved C-terminal catalytic domain, will give two bands of different apparent MW, the upper one DNMT3A1 and the lower one DNMT3A2;
* one pan-DNMT3B antibody directed to an epitope common to DNMT3B1 and DNMT3B3, e.g. the shared N-terminal region, will give two bands of different apparent MW, the upper one DNMT3B1 and the lower one DNMT3B3;
* one DNMT3L-specific antibody will give the single DNMT3L band.

The three immunoreactivities are sufficient to assign the five species on a size separated blot. Strictly isoform-unique reactivity with no reliance on mobility would require five isoform-specific antisera, one for each DNMT3A1, DNMT3A2, DNMT3B1, DNMT3B3 and DNMT3L.

